  <h2>Regional Trade Routes</h2>
  <p>
    BARK sits on the La Paz&ndash;Yungas corridor with access to Bolivia's main economic hubs:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>La Paz</strong> — 3&ndash;3.5 hours by road. Primary market for all goods. <strong>Mercado Rodriguez</strong> (5 km from city center) is the main wholesale market for Yungas agricultural products. International airport for exports.</li>
    <li><strong>Cochabamba</strong> — 8&ndash;10 hours by road (via La Paz or Oruro). Bolivia's agricultural processing center and the country's second-largest consumer market.</li>
    <li><strong>Santa Cruz</strong> — 12&ndash;14 hours by road. Bolivia's commercial capital and primary <strong>export gateway</strong> with the largest airport and connections to Brazilian logistics networks.</li>
    <li><strong>El Alto</strong> — Adjacent to La Paz. Bolivia's fastest-growing city and a major hub for informal trade, manufacturing, and the country's largest open-air market (Feria 16 de Julio).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Value-Added Processing</h2>
  <p>
    Raw materials leave as finished products wherever possible. Coffee is roasted and branded. Cacao becomes chocolate.
    Fruit becomes preserves and dried products. Essential oils are distilled and bottled. Each processing step
    adds 3&ndash;10x value and builds capability within the community. The <a href="/en/pillars/technopark">Techno Park</a> provides
    the machinery; the Trading House provides the market access.
  </p>

  <h2>The Reinvestment Loop</h2>
  <p>
    Profits from the spread between local purchase prices and international processed-good prices — plus margins
    on tech and industrial equipment sold to the region — flow back into infrastructure that improves community life.
    Better processing equipment means higher prices for farmers. Better solar installations mean lower energy costs
    for everyone. Better water systems mean healthier families. The economic engine feeds the community, and the
    community feeds the economic engine.
  </p>

  <h2>Economic Integration</h2>
  <p>
    BARK doesn't compete with the local economy — it amplifies it. The Trading House creates channels that
    local producers can also use. Shared logistics, shared market access, shared learning.
    A rising tide approach. Local coffee farmers accessing specialty export markets through BARK's channels
    benefits everyone.
  </p>

  <h2>Financial Resilience</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Multi-currency operations</strong> — Boliviano (BOB) for local trade, USD for international commerce, crypto capabilities for cross-border payments avoiding banking friction</li>
    <li><strong>Barter networks</strong> — For crisis scenarios and local exchange where cash is unnecessary</li>
    <li><strong>Cooperative treasury</strong> — Managed under Bolivian cooperative law (Ley 356) with transparent accounting and member oversight</li>
  </ul>

